\paragraph{Dose-response and sparsity limits.}
\label{sec:sparsity_cliff}
The CKA coefficient $\alpha$ continuously controls recovery capacity.
On GSM8K, recovery follows an inverted-U across $\alpha \in \{0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0\}$, peaking at $\alpha{=}2.0$ (SFT 64.97\%, GRPO 66.11\%; Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}, Table~\ref{tab:alpha}) with $|\Delta| \leq 1.14$\,pp at every point; $\alpha{=}1.0$ collapses (peak reward 0.061) while $\alpha{=}4.0$ recovers to 62.77\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_alpha}).\footnote{$\alpha{=}1.0$ uses \texttt{max\_completion\_length}{$=$}1{,}024; other $\alpha$ values use the TRL default of 512. Since $\alpha{=}1.0$ is the curve minimum, this makes the inverted-U a conservative estimate.}
The key finding generalizes to MATH-500: $\alpha{=}1.0$ remains the worst (GRPO 23.0\%), while higher-$\alpha$ and CKA-only methods cluster near the top (GRPO 27.2--27.6\%), though the inverted-U shape is not resolved at this difficulty level (Table~\ref{tab:math500}; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_math500}).
All methods collapse at 30\% sparsity (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sparsity_cliff}); pruning reduces parameters by 19\% and memory by ${\sim}$28\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_inference}).

